  "helpSearchHint": "Search help topics",
  "helpEmptySearchResult": "No results found",
  "helpStartSectionTitle": "Getting Started",
  "helpStartOnboardingTitle": "The screens you see on first launch",
  "helpStartOnboardingBody": "When you first open the app, it walks you through permissions like notifications, exact alarms, and display-over-other-apps. These are required for alarms to fire at the exact time even on the lock screen — skipping any of them can make alarms fire late or not at all.\n\nOnce every permission is granted, you'll move on to creating your work schedule. If the device already has a backup file (e.g. after reinstalling), the restore screen appears instead of onboarding.",
  "helpStartScheduleTypesTitle": "Repeating vs. irregular schedules",
  "helpStartScheduleTypesBody": "You can build your work schedule in two ways.\n\nA repeating schedule follows a fixed pattern — like \"day, day, night, night, off, off, rest\" — that keeps repeating from a chosen start date. Once you set the pattern and start date, the app fills in every future date automatically.\n\nAn irregular schedule has no fixed repeating pattern, so you assign a shift to each date yourself. Just tap a date on the calendar and pick the shift for that day.",
  "helpStartChangeLanguageTitle": "Changing the app language",
  "helpStartChangeLanguageBody": "You can switch the app language between Korean and English from the Settings tab. This changes not just the screen text but also the alarm screen and home-screen widget text.",
  "helpShiftSectionTitle": "Managing Shifts",
  "helpShiftIrregularAssignTitle": "Assigning shifts as an irregular worker",
  "helpShiftIrregularAssignBody": "If you're on an irregular schedule, tap a date in the Calendar tab and choose which shift you're working that day. This applies only to that one date, not a repeating pattern — you set each date individually.\n\nIf you assign shifts a few days ahead of time, alarms for those days are prepared automatically based on the assignment.",
  "helpShiftChangeShiftTitle": "Changing the shift on a specific date",
  "helpShiftChangeShiftBody": "Whether you're on a repeating or irregular schedule, you can override a single date by tapping it on the calendar and picking a different shift. Alarms for that day are automatically recalculated to match the new shift.",
  "helpShiftShiftColorsTitle": "Customizing shift colors",
  "helpShiftShiftColorsBody": "Every calendar theme has default colors per shift, but you can override the color of a specific shift name if you like. Pick the shift name in the shift settings and choose a color — your choice takes priority over the theme's default.",
  "helpShiftAllShiftsTitle": "What is the \"all shifts\" view?",
  "helpShiftAllShiftsBody": "This view shows the shift pattern of coworkers on other teams at the same workplace — handy for checking things like \"when is Team A off this week?\"\n\nYou can open it from the Calendar tab. For it to show correctly, you need to set up your team structure first (how many teams there are, and how many days ahead or behind each one rotates relative to you).",
  "helpShiftTeamChangeTitle": "When your team assignment changes",
  "helpShiftTeamChangeBody": "If your team assignment changes (e.g. due to a reassignment), just update your team info in the shift settings screen. Your existing shift names and alarm templates stay as they are — you only need to reset the pattern or reference date on top of them.",
  "helpAlarmSectionTitle": "Alarms",
  "helpAlarmTemplatesTitle": "Creating alarm templates",
  "helpAlarmTemplatesBody": "You can register up to 5 alarms per shift. For example, a night shift could have both a \"get ready\" alarm and a \"leave now\" alarm. Each alarm has its own time, sound, and repeat setting.",
  "helpAlarmDayOffsetTitle": "What do \"day before / same day / next day\" alarms mean?",
  "helpAlarmDayOffsetBody": "You can set an alarm to fire the evening before the shift, on the shift day itself, or the day after — relative to the date the shift is assigned to. For a night shift that starts in the early morning, you could set a \"9pm the day before\" reminder and an \"early morning of\" wake-up alarm.\n\nIf alarms from different shifts land on the exact same time, only one actually fires — same-day alarms take priority, then day-before, then day-after.",
  "helpAlarmPreAlertSnoozeTitle": "Pre-alert, snooze, and auto-stop",
  "helpAlarmPreAlertSnoozeBody": "You'll get a heads-up notification 20 minutes before an alarm fires. Once it rings, you can snooze it in 5-minute increments, and if it's left unattended it automatically stops after a set duration (to save battery and avoid endless noise).",
  "helpAlarmSoundVolumeTitle": "Changing alarm sounds / volume issues",
  "helpAlarmSoundVolumeBody": "You can choose from 7 built-in alarm sounds or your device's own system alarm sounds. Speaker characteristics vary by device, so the app auto-corrects volume, but if it still feels off, try switching to a different sound in the alarm settings.",
  "helpAlarmAlarmNotRingingTitle": "If an alarm doesn't go off",
  "helpAlarmAlarmNotRingingBody": "The most common cause is permissions. Check that \"exact alarms\", \"notifications\", and \"display over other apps\" are all enabled for this app, and that it's excluded from battery optimization (some manufacturers' battery savers block alarms outright).\n\nThe next most common cause is the shift or alarm setup itself — make sure a shift is actually assigned to that date, and that the shift has an alarm template registered. Assigning a shift without an alarm template means no alarm will fire.",
  "helpAlarmAlarmHistoryTitle": "Viewing alarm history",
  "helpAlarmAlarmHistoryBody": "A permanent record of when each alarm fired and how it was handled (dismissed, snoozed, or timed out) is kept. You can view it from the Settings tab under alarm history.",
  "helpAlarmPermissionToggleTitle": "If you turned Exact Alarm permission off then back on",
  "helpAlarmPermissionToggleBody": "If an alarm refresh happens to be running at the exact moment you turn \"Exact Alarm\" permission off and back on, a very small chance exists that a few alarms don't get re-armed correctly. If alarms feel off after toggling the permission, just open any screen and close it again, or fully quit and reopen the app — that forces alarms to refresh from scratch.",
  "helpCalendarSectionTitle": "Calendar & Home Widget",
  "helpCalendarThemesTitle": "Changing the calendar theme",
  "helpCalendarThemesBody": "You can choose from 9 calendar color themes. The choice applies not just inside the app's calendar but also to the home-screen widget.",
  "helpCalendarMemoTitle": "Adding a memo to a date",
  "helpCalendarMemoBody": "Long-press a date on the calendar (or open its detail view) to leave a memo for that day. This is separate from the Schedule tab's items — memos are simple day-level notes, while schedule items are tied to a specific time.",
  "helpCalendarOtTitle": "Recording and checking overtime",
  "helpCalendarOtBody": "From a date's detail view you can log overtime or extra shifts worked that day. What you log also feeds into the work-hours and pay calculations.",
  "helpCalendarWorkHoursPayTitle": "Checking work hours and estimated pay",
  "helpCalendarWorkHoursPayBody": "If you enter each shift's clock-in/out times and your hourly wage in settings, the app calculates and shows how many hours you actually worked in a month and your estimated pay, overtime included.",
  "helpCalendarHomeWidgetTitle": "Adding the home-screen widget",
  "helpCalendarHomeWidgetBody": "Long-press your home screen, open the widget picker, and add this app's calendar widget. It shows shift colors and memos, and follows whatever theme you've set inside the app.",
  "helpScheduleTabSectionTitle": "Schedule Tab",
  "helpScheduleTabWhatIsItTitle": "How is the Schedule tab different?",
  "helpScheduleTabWhatIsItBody": "While a calendar memo is a note for the whole day, the Schedule tab lets you manage items on a vertical timeline — what you're doing from one specific time to another. It's for things with an actual time attached, like an appointment before work or plans after your shift.",
  "helpScheduleTabCreateEditTitle": "Creating and editing a schedule item",
  "helpScheduleTabCreateEditBody": "Tap a spot on the timeline to create a schedule item, and tap an existing one to edit its time or content. Because it's placed directly on the timeline, you can fine-tune the time down to 5-minute increments.",
  "helpScheduleTabAutoCategoryTitle": "What is automatic category detection?",
  "helpScheduleTabAutoCategoryBody": "If you don't pick an icon yourself when creating an item, the app reads what you typed and automatically attaches a matching category icon (exercise, hospital, shopping, etc). This runs entirely on your device with no internet connection, and if you ever pick an icon manually, your choice always takes priority.",
  "helpConditionSectionTitle": "Condition Manager",
  "helpConditionWhatIsItTitle": "What is the Condition tab?",
  "helpConditionWhatIsItBody": "This tab analyzes your work schedule and gives you advice about sleep and fatigue load. It never shows numeric scores, percentages, or anything resembling a medical diagnosis — only three plain levels: Normal, Caution, and High Load. Every judgment is grounded in actual sleep-medicine and occupational-health research.",
  "helpConditionTodayForecastTitle": "What is \"Today's Condition Forecast\"?",
  "helpConditionTodayForecastBody": "This is the card at the top of the Condition tab. It combines your recent weeks of shift patterns with any actual sleep records you have and summarizes today's likely condition in 2-3 sentences — also factoring in whether today is unusually demanding compared to your own typical pattern.",
  "helpConditionHowJudgedTitle": "How is the condition level decided?",
  "helpConditionHowJudgedBody": "5 signals decide the level — short recovery time (under 11 hours), a long shift (12+ hours), heavy overtime in the last 7 days (8+ hours), a backward shift rotation, and 3+ consecutive night shifts. Any one of these raises the day to \"Attention\", and two or more overlapping pushes it to \"High load\".\n\nTwo of these are strong enough evidence to reach \"High load\" entirely on their own, with no other signal needed — long shifts repeating for 3+ days with under 48 hours of rest afterward, and 4+ consecutive night shifts (research shows risk accelerates sharply from that point on).\n\nExactly which research each signal cites is listed under \"Condition judgment evidence\" below. Info without a matching evidence entry, like a raw consecutive-work-day count, still never affects the level — it only appears as context.",
  "helpConditionEvidenceListTitle": "Condition judgment evidence",
  "helpConditionEvidenceListBody": "Every judgment and tip in the Condition tab cites at least one of these 13 evidence entries (always traceable via evidenceIds) — nothing is judged without a cited source.\n\nEVIDENCE-001 · Shift rotation direction — Forward rotation (day→evening→night) is reported to be easier to adapt to than backward rotation. (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-002 · Minimum rest between shifts — Recommends at least 11 hours of rest between two shifts. (Knauth & Hornberger, cited in NIOSH Module 5)\n\nEVIDENCE-003 · Minimum rest between shifts (legal standard) — Every worker must get at least 11 consecutive hours of rest per 24 hours. (EU Working Time Directive, Art. 3)\n\nEVIDENCE-004 · Long shifts and injury/accident risk — Relative risk is modeled at about +13% for a 10-hour shift and +27% for a 12-hour shift, versus an 8-hour shift. (Folkard & Lombardi, 2006)\n\nEVIDENCE-005 · Rest days after extended long shifts — Suggests at least 2 days of rest after 3 consecutive days of 12+ hour shifts. (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-006 · Sleep after a night shift — Recommends prioritizing sleep after a night shift. (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-007 · Napping before a night shift — Conditionally helpful, but the guideline itself states the evidence level is very low. Watch for grogginess right after waking. (AASM, 2025)\n\nEVIDENCE-008 · Caffeine and sleep — Caffeine taken even 6 hours before bedtime significantly cuts total sleep time by over an hour. (Drake et al., 2013)\n\nEVIDENCE-009 · Light exposure on the commute home after a night shift — Reducing bright light exposure can help later sleep (but safety comes first if you're too drowsy to drive safely). (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-010 · Eating during a night shift — Recommends eating less between midnight and 6am and favoring light meals. (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-011 · Recommended sleep for adults — Adults aged 18–64 are recommended 7–9 hours of sleep. (National Sleep Foundation, 2015)\n\nEVIDENCE-012 · Maximum weekly working hours (legal standard) — Average weekly working time, including overtime, must not exceed 48 hours. (EU Working Time Directive, Art. 6)\n\nEVIDENCE-013 · Consecutive night shifts and accident risk — Relative risk of accidents/injuries accumulates with each successive night shift (about +6% on night 2, +17% on night 3, +36% on night 4 — the increase is steeper for consecutive night shifts than for consecutive day/evening shifts). (Folkard & Tucker, 2003)",
  "helpConditionSetupNeededTitle": "Setup required before using this tab",
  "helpConditionSetupNeededBody": "You need to enter clock-in/out times for at least one shift name under Settings → Work Hours & OT first. Until you do, the Condition tab shows only a guidance message and no analysis. You don't need to fill in every shift — just one you're currently using is enough.",
  "helpConditionSameStartEndTitle": "A note on entering clock-in/out times",
  "helpConditionSameStartEndBody": "When entering clock-in/out times under Settings → Work Hours & OT, if you accidentally enter the same time for both, it gets calculated as a \"24-hour shift\" (because an end time that's earlier than or equal to the start time is read as crossing midnight). That can make the Condition tab judge an ordinary shift as far more demanding than it really is and show exaggerated warnings — always make sure the two times are different.",
  "helpSleepSectionTitle": "Sleep Tracking",
  "helpSleepWidgetTitle": "What is the sleep-tracking widget?",
  "helpSleepWidgetBody": "This is a separate widget from the home-screen calendar widget. It shows today's shift and your current state (active / sleeping / estimated sleeping), with \"🌙 Sleep\" and \"☀️ Wake\" buttons to log when you go to bed and get up.",
  "helpSleepAutoDetectTitle": "Automatic sleep estimation",
  "helpSleepAutoDetectBody": "Even without touching the widget, the app can guess \"you were probably asleep\" based on your work schedule and long stretches of phone inactivity. This is always just an estimate, though — you need to confirm it (\"That's right\") before it becomes an official record. To save battery, auto-detection only runs during your likely main sleep window (starting an hour before a night shift ends, or after 9pm otherwise) — short naps during a shift aren't covered and must be logged manually from the widget.",
  "helpSleepMiniCalendarTitle": "Reviewing and editing recent sleep records",
  "helpSleepMiniCalendarBody": "The \"Recent Sleep\" mini calendar on the Condition tab shows confirmed records day by day. Tap a slot to create, edit, or delete a record right there. For older records, use \"View All\" to browse month by month.",
  "helpFriendSectionTitle": "Friend Sharing",
  "helpFriendWhatIsItTitle": "What is friend sharing?",
  "helpFriendWhatIsItBody": "This lets you share your work schedule so someone else can view it — or view someone else's schedule in your own app — whether or not you work together. Handy for lining up days off with each other.",
  "helpFriendShareCodeTitle": "Generating and sharing your code",
  "helpFriendShareCodeBody": "Open \"My Share Code\" from settings to generate a code. Give it to the other person, and they can register it in their own app to view your schedule.",
  "helpFriendViewFriendTitle": "Viewing a friend's schedule with their code",
  "helpFriendViewFriendBody": "If you've received a friend's code, add them from the friend list screen by entering it — their schedule then shows up as a calendar inside your own app.",
  "helpFriendReinstallNoteTitle": "What happens if you reinstall the app",
  "helpFriendReinstallNoteBody": "Reinstalling the app creates what's internally treated as a new account, which breaks any friend-sharing connections you had before. To reconnect, generate a new share code and send it to the other person again.\n\nYour friend won't see any error on their end — their screen will just keep showing your schedule exactly as it was right before you reinstalled. Since they have no way to notice this on their own, be sure to let them know if you reinstall.",
  "helpBackupSectionTitle": "Backup & Restore",
  "helpBackupDataStorageTitle": "Where is my data stored?",
  "helpBackupDataStorageBody": "Things like alarms, your work schedule, memos, schedule items, and sleep records are stored only on your own device. None of it is sent to an external server, only you can access it, and it's gone if you delete the app — which is exactly why backups matter.\n\nThe one exception is if you turn on friend sharing — in that case only the minimum needed to share (shift pattern, colors) is uploaded to a server (Firestore). Memos, overtime, work hours, and alarm settings are never uploaded, even then.",
  "helpBackupAutoBackupTitle": "When does automatic backup happen?",
  "helpBackupAutoBackupBody": "Whenever you leave the app (pressing home or switching apps), it backs up automatically — but only if something actually changed since the last backup. It's saved to the Download/ShiftBell folder on your device; it's not sent to the cloud or another device automatically. You can also back up manually anytime via \"Back Up Now\" in settings.",
  "helpBackupRestoreTitle": "Restoring after reinstalling or moving to a new device",
  "helpBackupRestoreBody": "After a fresh install, if a backup file exists on the device, the restore screen appears automatically on first launch. To move to a new device, first copy the backup file from the old device's Download/ShiftBell folder into the same folder on the new device, then install the app.\n\nAfter restoring, permissions like notifications and exact alarms are asked for again as a safety measure.",
  "helpBackupRestoreManualTitle": "If automatic restore doesn't find your backup",
  "helpBackupRestoreManualBody": "On some devices (certain Samsung models in particular), automatic detection can fail to find a backup that's actually there. Use the \"Load manually\" link on the restore screen to open a file picker and select the backup file from the Download/ShiftBell folder yourself.",
  "helpBackupRestoreOldBackupFailTitle": "A very old backup may fail to restore",
  "helpBackupRestoreOldBackupFailBody": "The data format the app saves occasionally changes as it's updated. If there's a big version gap between when a backup was made and when you restore it (for example, restoring a backup made a long time ago into a much newer version of the app), the restore can fail. Retrying with the same file will likely fail again for the same reason. To avoid this, it's a good idea to tap \"Back up now\" in Settings right before switching phones or reinstalling, to make a fresh backup — even if automatic backups have been working fine, doing one manually right before an important moment adds an extra layer of safety.",
  "helpTroubleshootSectionTitle": "Troubleshooting",
  "helpTroubleshootAlarmIssueTitle": "My alarm didn't go off",
  "helpTroubleshootAlarmIssueBody": "First check that notifications, exact alarms, and display-over-other-apps are enabled for this app, and that it's excluded from battery optimization. Then confirm a shift is actually assigned to that date and that the shift has an alarm template registered.",
  "helpTroubleshootWidgetIssueTitle": "The widget isn't updating",
  "helpTroubleshootWidgetIssueBody": "Try removing the widget and adding it again. If that doesn't help, simply opening the app once (bringing it to the foreground is one of the triggers that refreshes the widget) usually fixes it.",
  "helpTroubleshootBackupFileMissingTitle": "I can't find my backup file",
  "helpTroubleshootBackupFileMissingBody": "Check the Download/ShiftBell folder directly using your device's file manager app. If the folder doesn't exist, no automatic backup has run yet — we recommend tapping \"Back Up Now\" in settings to create one.",
  "helpTroubleshootFriendDisconnectedTitle": "My friend's schedule hasn't changed in days",
  "helpTroubleshootFriendDisconnectedBody": "If the schedule just looks frozen with no error shown, it's almost always because your friend reinstalled the app. Reinstalling issues a new internal account, which breaks the existing connection — but nothing on screen tells you that happened, it just keeps showing whatever it last had.\n\nCheck the \"Last synced\" time at the top of your friend's schedule screen — if it's stuck on an old date, that's the sign. Ask your friend for a new share code, then remove the old entry and add them again with it."
}
